    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "It is important to note that the above expressions refer to randomly chosen markers. It is much more efficient to use sets of equally spaced markers. If the entire genome is covered by marker loci equally spaced at m map units apart, no locus is more than m/2 from any marker, and the average distance of a locus from a marker (assuming a uniform distribution of loci along the chromosome) is m/4.",
      "source_page": 411,
      "printed_page": 391,
